  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Spatial Boundaries, Abounding Spaces shows early signs of reach rather than citation-based uptake: it has 1,502 recorded downloads, a small amount of public discussion captured through Crossref, and support from KU Leuven. The book is also openly available in gold open access, which likely helps explain its visible use.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Mohit Chandna\u2019s monograph explores how colonialism reshaped ideas of borders and space in French and Francophone literature and film, bringing together literary and visual analyses across a long historical arc. Its thematic profile is strongly aligned with colonialism and French studies, with additional resonance in history and the humanities.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest sign of uptake in the record is usage: the book has been downloaded 1,502 times through OAPEN, with activity appearing across the year rather than as a single isolated spike. That pattern suggests continuing interest from readers engaging with the open version of the work.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Public-facing attention is present but still limited in scale. The record shows two Crossref-linked mentions, indicating that the book has entered public discourse in some form, though the available data do not support a broader media or event narrative. This is best read as modest visibility rather than extensive reception.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication context also points to institutional support and accessibility. Leuven University Press published the book in a series, and the record notes KU Leuven support. OpenAIRE identifies the title as gold open access with multiple open instances, which strengthens discoverability and reduces barriers to readership.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the scholarly citation record remains very early, with OpenAlex showing no citations yet. That does not diminish the book\u2019s substantive contribution; it simply means that, in the evidence currently available, its footprint is defined more by access and use than by citation accumulation.</p>",
